{
  "query": "A media startup is launching a professional networking app where members connect, follow companies, and browse personalized activity streams. The team needs a data store that models highly connected relationships among people, follows, and posts while supporting horizontal scale for rapid growth to tens of millions of users. Which kind of database technology should they choose?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "A globally distributed relational database that provides strong consistency and horizontal scaling with SQL capabilities",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Document oriented NoSQL database",
      "explanation": "A store for flexible JSON like documents that works well when records have varying fields and schema evolution is frequent",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Graph NoSQL database engine",
      "explanation": "A database that models entities as nodes and relationships as edges to enable efficient traversal of interconnected data",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Key value NoSQL database",
      "explanation": "A store optimized for simple key based access that delivers very fast reads and writes for individual items",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Graph NoSQL database engine</b>.</p><p>A <b>graph database</b> models entities as nodes and their relationships as edges, which aligns directly with people, follow relationships, companies, and posts. Features like efficient multi hop traversals and adjacency queries make a <b>graph database</b> ideal for building feeds, recommendations, and mutual connection discovery. Many <b>graph databases</b> are designed for horizontal scale by distributing storage and computation across clusters, which supports rapid growth to tens of millions of users.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner</i> offers global relational consistency and strong scaling yet it is optimized for normalized relational schemas and SQL rather than pointer heavy traversals and repeated relationship hops. Modeling graph queries in SQL can be complex and inefficient for this use case.</p><p><i>Document oriented NoSQL database</i> works well for flexible document schemas and hierarchical data but it does not natively store or traverse relationships efficiently across many documents. Multi hop queries and graph style recommendations often require heavy denormalization and application side joins.</p><p><i>Key value NoSQL database</i> excels at simple lookups with very high throughput yet it lacks native graph structures and efficient traversal across connected entities. Implementing friend of friend and feed expansion logic becomes application heavy and inefficient.</p>",
